{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## The defect\n\n`rebar-observability.service` does not publish sparsely. It is **killed part-way through most runs\nunder load**, and every metric published after the kill point never appears at all.\n\nFrom the host journal, 2026-09-05:\n\n    18:25:50  Starting rebar-observability.service - rebar health + disk observability probe...\n    18:29:50  rebar-observability.service: Main process exited, code=killed, status=15/TERM\n    18:29:50  rebar-observability.service: Failed with result 'timeout'.\n    18:29:50  rebar-observability.service: Consumed 34.131s CPU time.\n\n`systemctl show` gives `TimeoutStartUSec = 4min`; the run spans **exactly** 18:25:50 -> 18:29:50.\nIt is not finishing late; it is SIGTERM-ed on the timeout. Frequency: **55 timeout kills against\n197 completed runs in 24h**, clustered under I/O load — i.e. when an operator most needs it.\n\n## The decisive measurement: 4 minutes wall-clock, ~35 seconds CPU\n\nConsumed CPU across observed runs: **34.131s** and **39.687s** against 4 minutes of wall time. The\nprobe spends ~3.4 minutes **BLOCKED**, not computing. Raising the timeout would only move the kill\npoint; making the script computationally faster would not help at all.\n\n## The mechanism is CUMULATIVE, not a single unbounded call\n\nAn early framing of this ticket named `docker_du_bytes`'s `du -sx` as an unbounded multi-minute\nblocking call. **That framing is wrong and is corrected here.** Every expensive call in\n`observability.sh` is ALREADY `timeout`-wrapped: `DOCKER_DU_TIMEOUT=120`, `DOCKER_DF_TIMEOUT=20`,\n`JOURNAL_DU_TIMEOUT=60`, `VAR_TMP_DU_TIMEOUT=60`, `JOURNAL_SCAN_TIMEOUT=10`, and literal `timeout 15`\non the docker/`git ls-remote` calls. There is no unbounded blocker to find.\n\nThe defect is that **nothing composes those bounds**. `docker_du_bytes` is called TWICE\n(observability.sh:592-593, over `$DOCKER_ROOT` and again over `$DOCKER_ROOT/overlay2` — essentially\nthe same ~1.88M-file tree), and 2 x 120 = **240s = `TimeoutStartSec` exactly**, before the journal,\nvar_tmp, container, memory or counter sections run at all. Summing every ceiling in the file gives\n**over 495s inside a 240s timeout**. Each ceiling carries a comment justifying itself against that\ntimeout; the journal section reasons \"12 scans x 10s = 120s < 240s\" while the docker section is\nseparately entitled to 240s of the same budget. Every ceiling is individually correct and the set is\nindividually wrong.\n\nSo the investigation must measure **cumulative section time against a global budget**, not hunt a\nsingle slow call.\n\n## Why this is the root cause of several open tickets\n\nMetrics publish sequentially, so a metric's POSITION decides whether it survives the kill:\n\n- `root_disk_used_percent` (line 192, early) — fresh, 10 points in 60 min. Healthy.\n- `docker_storage_bytes` (line 601, the first metric AFTER the walk) — 70% present, 35 min max gap.\n- `mem_used_percent` (line 1020, late) — newest datapoint 17:40Z, nothing for ~49 minutes.\n- `var_tmp_used_percent` (line 798) — **0% present**; never once reached.\n- `mirror_out_of_sync` / `g2p_dispatch_errors` (lines 1463/1511, last) — 43% present, 85 min gaps.\n\nOne mechanism explains three recorded mysteries: `illbred-sour-alpaca` (alarms firing on healthy\noperation — the empty buckets are the section never running), `enviable-beloved-buffalo` (\"three\nmetrics published nothing for 7+ days\" — never reached), and `herbal-garish-monkey`'s dead-man\ndesign being fed by a publisher that silently truncates itself.\n\n**A correction:** an earlier version of this ticket listed `gerrit_healthy` as \"later still — no\ndatapoints at all\". That is wrong. `gerrit_healthy` is published at **line 97, the EARLIEST of the\nthree**, and it survives the kill. Its apparent absence is a query-side artifact: it carries an\n`InstanceId` dimension while its sibling `GerritReachable` is dimensionless, and CloudWatch keys a\nmetric by namespace+name+dimensions. It is NOT evidence of truncation and must not be used as such.\n\nMeasured consequence: of six alarms in ALARM at 17:09 UTC, only **two** were genuine.\n`docker_unaccounted_bytes` read 1.87 GB against a 2 GiB threshold; BuildKit read 10% against 85.\n\n## Operator consequence, observed first-hand\n\nA session used `mem_used_percent` to decide when to restart a leaking MCP process. That metric is\nlate, so it went blind for 49 minutes while the leak continued. **A probe that truncates itself\nremoves exactly the signals an operator reaches for during pressure**, because pressure is what\nmakes it slow enough to be killed.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] Cumulative attribution by measurement: per-section elapsed time summed against the 240s\n      budget, showing which sections consume it. Account for the existing per-call `timeout` bounds\n      rather than seeking one unbounded call.\n- [ ] The sum of wall-clock bounds is provably below `TimeoutStartSec`, not merely observed to fit\n      once. State the guaranteed margin and how it is enforced.\n- [ ] The probe completes within its timeout under representative load over consecutive runs.\n- [ ] A truncated run cannot be silent: a killed or early-exiting run publishes that fact, so \"the\n      section never ran\" is distinguishable from \"the value is missing\". Today they are identical.\n- [ ] Metric publication no longer depends on position. **Committed mechanism: a reserved tail\n      budget that no earlier section can spend** — reordering alone only moves which metric starves.\n- [ ] Defect-seeded: force a call to exceed the budget and confirm the truncation signal fires and\n      the later metrics still publish.\n- [ ] Re-measure the alarm surface: the four alarms firing on gaps should clear without their\n      thresholds changing. If they do not, the diagnosis is incomplete.\n- [ ] Back-out path stated: this feeds live dead-man alarms, so the prior probe revision must be\n      revertible via the normal deploy path.\n- [ ] Portability: host shell + systemd + local validation; no dependence on any single CI provider.\n\n## Testing\n\n1. RED first: show a run exceeding 4 minutes with ~35s CPU, killed at the boundary.\n2. Attribute the wall clock in situ — the probe's own timestamped `logger -t rebar-health`\n   breadcrumbs sit at known line offsets, so interleaving them with the unit lifecycle brackets\n   each section without adding instrumentation.\n3. Confirm which metrics fall after the kill point and that they match the observed absences.\n4. After the fix, verify late-section metrics publish on consecutive runs under induced I/O load.\n\n## Provenance\n\nFound 2026-09-05 while investigating why `mem_used_percent` had stopped publishing. The gaps were\nrepeatedly mis-attributed to \"cadence jitter\" and \"sparse publishing\" because a truncated run and a\nslow publisher are indistinguishable from the metric side. The journal settles it.\n"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788646503732250001,"uuid":"2c5ce0b9-4f2a-432b-b03b-12956b38c0ef"}